  <section class="wrap fiche-body">
    <div>
      <h2>The vibe</h2>
      <p>A small rocky cove at the Pointe des Sardinaux, at the end of Avenue du Général Leclerc, just steps from the centre of Sainte-Maxime. The spot is popular with free-diving enthusiasts for its rocky-bottom fish, and remains a familiar meeting point for the town's dog owners, summer and winter alike.</p>

      <h2>What to know before you go</h2>
      <p>Unlike most of Sainte-Maxime's beaches, which only allow dogs off-season (1 October to 31 May), La Madrague and the land portion of the Pointe des Sardinaux are generally presented as the two coastal spots allowed for dogs year-round, on leash. We must, however, flag a contradiction found in our research: at least one source claims that the Madrague cove has no longer been dog-friendly year-round since 2025, with the permanent zone instead being a "dog beach" near the Pont du Préconil in the town centre, La Madrague only becoming tolerated again in low season (1 October-31 May) like La Croisette and Les Éléphants. Sainte-maxime.com is the tourist office's website, not the town hall's, and we did not find the municipal order online to settle this contradiction. Also watch out for seagrass (posidonia) in the water, which makes the seabed slippery in places.</p>
      <div class="callout">Coco's tip: if you're after fine sand instead, the town also tolerates dogs off-season on La Croisette and Les Éléphants beaches, just steps from the town centre.</div>

      <h2>Nearby</h2>
      <p>Sainte-Maxime's harbour and town centre are a few minutes' walk away, with plenty of seafront café terraces where your dog can join you.</p>
    </div>

